hing  about it  if  putin has a direct attack on a nato  country then there is an obligation to  do something about it and the  credibility of that obligation is  absolutely key what we will see is  attempts by putin to blur that  commitment of article 5 to try and cast  doubt on it by doing things that aren't  quite serious enough to  cause an article 5 event  but at the same time a very offensive  and erosive and if you do that enough  you start to kind of loosen  the  architecture of nato and then i think we  get into really really dangerous  territory because the calculation from  russia is that they can go farther and  intimidate everybody in article 5 really  doesn't matter very much but nature has  to demonstrate article 5 does and then  before you know it you're in a war  between nato and russia if russia is at  war with nato then i don't see how you  stop escalation getting getting really  really bad and  i i could see that easily ending in a  nuclear exchange this is one man  with more power than anybody had in  russia since stalin i mean stalin did  have pretty much the same amount of  power  paranoid about uh his security a man who  spent uh two years from what we  understand in fact in a bunker isolating  from from covet  we see his meetings with  foreign minister lavrov and his defense  minister shoigu seated about  looks like 20 meters apart  not just because of covered but because  the security services are paranoid about  the possibility of assassination attack  now what we also have known about putin  since the beginning is that he  is a man who  does not and never has  uh accepted his mistakes and never  admitted to them  we know that putin used to back off a  bit tactically he would then sort of go  back again but  this time he's not backing  at all and uh yes i i'm worried about  his state of mind and the uh this very  very small cabal of people around him  who now  have they know they're part of this they  know they're going all the way down wi